1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,938
Welcome to lesson seventeen: troubleshooting a cluster.

2
00:00:04,038 --> 00:00:14,160
You'll learn to fix problems methodically instead of guessing: a repeatable method, a decision tree for every Pod state, the debugging toolkit, and how to fix nodes and the control plane.

3
00:00:15,510 --> 00:00:16,816
Start with a method.

4
00:00:16,916 --> 00:00:24,878
Define exactly what's wrong and since when. Find the scope. Ask what changed recently. Follow the layers until you find where it breaks.

5
00:00:24,978 --> 00:00:32,220
Read the actual error message. Test one hypothesis at a time. Verify that the user's symptom is gone. And write it down.

6
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:37,642
Avoid restarting things at random. It hides the evidence and doesn't explain the cause.

7
00:00:38,992 --> 00:00:43,434
Work through the layers one at a time, from the user inward, or from the Pod outward.

8
00:00:43,534 --> 00:00:54,376
DNS and the load balancer, the Ingress, the Service and its endpoints, network policies, the Pod, the container, the node, the control plane, and finally the infrastructure.

9
00:00:55,726 --> 00:00:59,576
Start with kubectl get pods, then follow the status.

10
00:00:59,676 --> 00:01:05,398
Pending is a scheduling problem. If there are no events at all, the scheduler itself may be down.

11
00:01:05,498 --> 00:01:15,668
ContainerCreating stuck usually means a volume or network problem. ImagePullBackOff is an image problem. CreateContainerConfigError is a missing ConfigMap or Secret.

12
00:01:15,768 --> 00:01:24,738
CrashLoopBackOff means the app keeps exiting, so read the previous logs and the exit code. And Running but not ready means the readiness probe is failing.

13
00:01:26,088 --> 00:01:28,082
Exit codes tell you a lot.

14
00:01:28,182 --> 00:01:38,672
One twenty-seven means command not found. One thirty-seven means the process was killed, usually because it ran out of memory. One forty-three means a normal stop with SIGTERM.

15
00:01:38,772 --> 00:01:44,222
Zero means the process exited successfully, which is a problem for a server that should keep running.

16
00:01:45,572 --> 00:01:47,134
Here's the core toolkit.

17
00:01:47,234 --> 00:01:54,828
get with dash o wide, events sorted by time, describe, logs with dash dash previous, exec, port-forward, and top.

18
00:01:54,928 --> 00:02:00,042
Events are kept for only about an hour, so look quickly, or ship them to your logging system.

19
00:02:01,392 --> 00:02:05,210
Many production images have no shell, so exec fails.

20
00:02:05,310 --> 00:02:13,992
kubectl debug attaches an ephemeral container with its own tools, sharing the target's processes and network. Netshoot is a full network toolkit.

21
00:02:14,092 --> 00:02:19,318
It can also copy a crashing Pod with a different command, or give you a shell on a node.

22
00:02:20,668 --> 00:02:27,750
For a NotReady node, get a shell on it and check the kubelet's status and its logs, which usually say why it fails.

23
00:02:27,850 --> 00:02:32,196
Check the container runtime with crictl, and check disk space and memory.

24
00:02:32,296 --> 00:02:41,234
Common causes are a stopped kubelet, a stopped runtime, a full disk, an expired certificate, or network problems reaching the API server.

25
00:02:42,584 --> 00:02:45,106
The symptom often points to the component.

26
00:02:45,206 --> 00:02:56,160
If kubectl fails for everything, suspect the API server. Pending with no events, suspect the scheduler. A Deployment with no ReplicaSet, suspect the controller manager.

27
00:02:56,260 --> 00:03:04,766
On kubeadm clusters, these run as static Pods from files on the control-plane node. If the API server is down, use crictl there.

28
00:03:04,866 --> 00:03:10,572
On managed clusters, you can't edit the control plane. Check the provider's status instead.

29
00:03:11,922 --> 00:03:13,852
Sometimes things won't delete.

30
00:03:13,952 --> 00:03:25,594
A Pod stuck terminating usually sits on a node that's gone. Force-deleting is possible, but be careful with StatefulSet Pods: if the node is still running, you could end up with two Pods with the same identity.

31
00:03:25,694 --> 00:03:35,816
A namespace stuck terminating usually has custom resources whose operator was removed, or a broken aggregated API. Removing finalizers by hand skips their cleanup.

32
00:03:37,166 --> 00:03:44,360
Here's a worked example. Users get five oh three on slash api, starting just after a new version was deployed.

33
00:03:44,460 --> 00:03:52,934
The Ingress is fine. The Service has no endpoints. The Pods are running but not ready, and describe shows the readiness probe gets a four oh four.

34
00:03:53,034 --> 00:04:01,876
The new version moved its health endpoint. Roll back first to stop the errors, then fix the probe path properly. Notice that nothing was restarted at random.

35
00:04:03,226 --> 00:04:08,212
The lab gives you nine broken workloads to diagnose and fix, each a classic failure.

36
00:04:08,312 --> 00:04:16,322
You'll debug an image with no shell, stop a node's kubelet and bring it back, and take down the scheduler to see the pending with no events pattern.

37
00:04:17,672 --> 00:04:18,930
Let's recap.

38
00:04:19,030 --> 00:04:23,120
Follow the method, and let the Pod status point you to the first command.

39
00:04:23,220 --> 00:04:30,910
Know your exit codes, use kubectl debug when there's no shell, and check the kubelet, runtime and disk on broken nodes.

40
00:04:31,010 --> 00:04:37,388
That completes level three. Next, level four begins with a deep dive into how the cluster really works.
